From 59434bf8595cdd6eaf7907ca3060e6fefad10ed7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Contributor <contributor@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 07:45:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(frontend): generate API types from the OpenAPI spec
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

PARKED.md names this the cheapest parked item and the only one with a standing
cost: docs/openapi.json is already exported, nothing consumes it, and
frontend/src/lib/api.ts carries ~50 hand-written interfaces that drift by
construction. This wires up the generation and migrates what the spec can
actually support today.

The exported spec turned out to be too narrow to use as-is. web/app.py hides
every route and re-enables a 16-entry _PUBLIC_API allowlist, so
docs/openapi.json carries 12 paths out of the 427 routes the app registers —
generating from it produces 975 lines that cannot type the portal client.
scripts/export_openapi.py gains --internal, which lifts the filter and exports
351 paths / 294 schemas to frontend/openapi.json (gitignored build artifact).
The public spec stays byte-identical; verified by re-exporting and diffing.

16 of the 46 hand-written interfaces in api.ts become aliases into the
generated schemas:

    export type DashboardStats = components["schemas"]["DashboardStatsResponse"]

DashboardStats alone is 17 fields that previously had to be kept in sync by
hand. ConnectorCallStat / ConnectorActionStat are deleted: the generated
ConnectorStatsResponse references identically-shaped generated versions, which
is what made them dead.

The other 30 stay hand-written, for two concrete reasons rather than neglect:

- No schema exists. ~13 of them back endpoints that declare no response_model
  (AdminEvalDataset, AdminEvalRun, AdminSchedule, AdminCredential, AdminReview,
  MarketItem, UserOrg, OrgMember, AdminWorkflowInfo, ...), so the spec emits
  nothing to alias. Adding response_model is the unblocking change.
- A second hand-written copy exists in src/types/admin.ts with different
  optionality (AdminAgentInfo, AdminApiKeyInfo, AdminKBDoc,
  AdminLoginHistoryEntry, AdminKBDetail). Aliasing those made tsc fail at
  admin-agents.tsx:98, admin-api-keys.tsx:112/140,
  admin-knowledge-bases.tsx:184 and admin-security.tsx:143 — the duplication is
  the real obstacle, and collapsing it is a separate change.

Generating the types also surfaced live drift the hand-written interfaces were
hiding. These are NOT fixed here (they change what the admin UI renders, which
is the maintainer's call); reported with the exact lines instead:

- admin-analytics.tsx:427,601 read item.owner; the model field is owner_username
  (admin_analytics.py:57,77) — both cells always render "--".
- admin-analytics.tsx:428 reads item.conversations; the field is
  total_conversations (:58) — always renders 0.
- admin-analytics.tsx:430 reads item.avg_tokens_per_conv; the field is
  avg_tokens_per_conversation (:60) — always renders 0.
- admin-analytics.tsx:522 reads item.errors; the field is error_count (:68) —
  always renders 0.
- admin-skills.tsx:270 reads skill.agents_using; AdminSkillInfo has agent_count
  (admin_skills.py:49) — column always blank.
- admin-skills.tsx:369,373 read detailTarget.system_prompt; AdminSkillDetail has
  no such field (admin_skills.py:53-57) — block never renders.

Verification:
  pnpm exec tsc --noEmit   -> clean
  pnpm lint                -> 0 errors (13 pre-existing warnings, 2 fewer than
                              before: the dead interfaces are gone)
  pnpm test                -> 15 files, 161 passed (6 new)
  pnpm build               -> succeeds; in this sandbox next/font cannot reach
                              fonts.googleapis.com, so the build was verified
                              with layout.tsx temporarily pointed at the
                              repo's local font and then reverted
